    "parsing_res_list": [
        {
            "block_label": "header",
            "block_content": "UFC 3-340-025 December 2008Change 2,1 September 2014",
            "block_bbox": [
                731,
                147,
                1050,
                233
            ],
            "block_id": 0,
            "block_order": null
        },
        {
            "block_label": "paragraph_title",
            "block_content": "DYNAMIC DESIGN OF EXTERIOR COLUMNS ",
            "block_bbox": [
                344,
                300,
                840,
                322
            ],
            "block_id": 1,
            "block_order": 1
        },
        {
            "block_label": "paragraph_title",
            "block_content": "4-50INTRODUCTION.",
            "block_bbox": [
                134,
                352,
                464,
                373
            ],
            "block_id": 2,
            "block_order": 2
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "Exterior columns may be required for severe loading conditions. These columns could be monolithic with the exterior walls and would be subjected to both axial and transverse loading. The axial load is due to floor and roof beam reactions, while the transverse load is due to the direct impact of the blast load.\n",
            "block_bbox": [
                133,
                397,
                1025,
                500
            ],
            "block_id": 3,
            "block_order": 3
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "The use of exterior columns would normally be restricted to use in framed structures to transfer roof and floor beam reactions to the foundation. Normally, only tied columns would be used since they are compatible with the placement of wall and beam reinforcement. Exterior columns are not normally required for flat slab structures since roof and floor loads are supported by the exterior walls.\n",
            "block_bbox": [
                133,
                522,
                1032,
                651
            ],
            "block_id": 4,
            "block_order": 4
        },
        {
            "block_label": "paragraph_title",
            "block_content": "4-51DESIGN OF EXTERIOR COLUMNS.",
            "block_bbox": [
                134,
                674,
                663,
                695
            ],
            "block_id": 5,
            "block_order": 5
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "Exterior columns are generally designed as beam elements. The axial load on these columns may be significant, but usually the effect of the transverse load is greater. The column will usually be in the tension controls region (e greater than $\\mathbf{e}_{b})$ of the P-M curve (Figure 4-61 (+)) where the addition of axial load increases the moment capacity of the member.\n",
            "block_bbox": [
                133,
                719,
                1042,
                847
            ],
            "block_id": 6,
            "block_order": 6
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "Consequently, the design of an exterior column as a beam, where the axial load is neglected, is conservative.\n",
            "block_bbox": [
                133,
                871,
                987,
                919
            ],
            "block_id": 7,
            "block_order": 7
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "Since an exterior column is a primary member that is subjected to an axial load, it is not permitted to attain large plastic deformations. Therefore, the lateral deflection of exterior columns must be limited to a maximum ductility $(X_{m}/X_{E})$ of 3.\n",
            "block_bbox": [
                133,
                941,
                1043,
                1018
            ],
            "block_id": 8,
            "block_order": 8
        },
        {
            "block_label": "number",
            "block_content": "4-194",
            "block_bbox": [
                568,
                1485,
                628,
                1504
            ],
            "block_id": 9,
            "block_order": null
        }
    ],
